WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.347
Containment is the work of limiting damage while preserving enough evidence and business continuity to make good decisions.

2
00:00:07.467 --> 00:00:16.038
It is one of the hardest parts of incident response because the best action depends on timing, confidence, impact, and risk.

3
00:00:16.158 --> 00:00:23.913
A playbook should give responders containment options and decision criteria, not just a single command to run every time.

4
00:00:24.033 --> 00:00:37.503
Common containment options include isolating a host, disabling an account, revoking tokens, blocking an indicator, quarantining email, restricting a network path, pausing an exposed service, removing public access, or temporarily limiting a role.

5
00:00:37.623 --> 00:00:45.786
Each action has tradeoffs. Isolating a host may stop activity but can disrupt business work and may change volatile evidence.

6
00:00:45.906 --> 00:00:54.069
Disabling an account may prevent unauthorized access but can affect critical service operations if the account is used by automation.

7
00:00:54.189 --> 00:00:59.496
Blocking an indicator may reduce noise but may not address the root cause.

8
00:00:59.616 --> 00:01:06.146
Pausing an exposed service may protect data but can affect customers. Premature containment can destroy context.

9
00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:14.429
If responders immediately power off a system, they may lose memory, network state, active process information, or live session details.

10
00:01:14.549 --> 00:01:22.304
If they remove every suspicious email before measuring scope, they may make it harder to know who received it.

11
00:01:22.424 --> 00:01:30.588
If they revoke a cloud key without capturing audit evidence, they may lose a clean view of what it accessed.

12
00:01:30.708 --> 00:01:43.361
This does not mean containment should be delayed by default. It means the playbook should help responders decide what evidence is needed before action, and when urgent risk overrides further collection.

13
00:01:43.481 --> 00:01:51.644
Delayed containment can increase impact. If a privileged account is actively changing policies, waiting too long can expose more resources.

14
00:01:51.764 --> 00:01:57.478
If a device is still communicating with known malicious infrastructure, isolation may be urgent.

15
00:01:57.598 --> 00:02:02.904
If sensitive data is publicly reachable, reducing exposure may be the first priority.

16
00:02:03.024 --> 00:02:10.371
The playbook should identify conditions that justify fast containment and the approvals required when business impact is high.

17
00:02:10.491 --> 00:02:18.655
Eradication comes after the team understands enough to remove the attacker's foothold, malicious artifacts, persistence, unauthorized access, and exploited weakness.

18
00:02:18.775 --> 00:02:34.285
In defensive terms, eradication may mean removing malware, resetting credentials, rotating keys, deleting unauthorized accounts, closing an exposed service path, fixing a vulnerable application, removing a malicious mailbox rule, or rebuilding a compromised host from a trusted image.

19
00:02:34.405 --> 00:02:40.119
The key is that eradication addresses more than the symptom. Recovery restores systems safely.

20
00:02:40.239 --> 00:02:52.076
The playbook should require verification: access reviewed, persistence removed, logs checked, systems patched or rebuilt, data restored if needed, monitoring in place, and owners ready to resume normal operation.

21
00:02:52.196 --> 00:02:59.135
Containment, eradication, and recovery are connected decisions. A strong playbook helps responders act quickly without acting blindly.
